Operator Precedence in MySQL

Parentheses (...)
Always evaluated first, no matter what.
Example: (2+3)*4 = 20, not 2+(3*4).


Unary operators
- (negative), ! (logical NOT), ~ (bitwise NOT).
Example: -5 + 3 = -2.



Arithmetic operators
*, /, % (multiplication, division, modulo).
Then +, - (addition, subtraction).
Example: 10 + 2 * 3 = 16 (not 36).



Bitwise shift operators
<<, >>
Example: 4 << 1 = 8.



Comparison operators
=, <=>, >, <, >=, <=, <>, !=, BETWEEN, IN, LIKE, REGEXP.



Example: 5 > 3+4 → 5 > 7 → false.
IS operators
IS NULL, IS NOT NULL, IS TRUE, IS FALSE.
Example: NULL IS NULL → true.



NOT (logical)
Example: NOT (5 > 3) → false.

AND (logical)
Example: (5 > 3) AND (2 > 4) → false.

XOR
Example: 1 XOR 0 → 1, 1 XOR 1 → 0.



OR (logical)
Lowest precedence.
Example: (5 > 3) OR (2 > 4) → true.
